WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:07.420
Did you know that just this year, paleontologists
have discovered new insights into the behavior

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:12.490
and appearance of Spinosaurus, the biomechanics
of Megaladon shark teeth, and the strangest

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:17.830
early lifeforms of the Cambrian Explosion?
New revelations are occurring all the time

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:22.940
regarding Earth’s distant past, yet these
incredible findings often go unnoticed by

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:29.240
the general public. So, this year, I’m taking
part in PaleoRewind, a breakdown of some of

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:34.290
the most important revelations in the field
of Paleontology from the past year. All sorts

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:39.770
of other creators you might already be subscribed
to are also participating. In this video,

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:44.450
I’ll kick the rewind off by covering findings
from the first half of January — but be

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:49.550
sure to check out everyone else’s entries
too, and the final 2021 compilation video

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:55.530
that will be uploaded on E.D.G.E.’s channel.
Now let’s get started… Beginning in early

00:00:55.530 --> 00:01:01.760
January 2021, we have new findings on the
potential ecology and feeding behavior of

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:07.259
the famous theropod ‘Spinosarus.’ The
Spinosarus is a dinosaur that has long gripped

00:01:07.259 --> 00:01:12.149
popular culture in its elongated jaws thanks
in part to its appearance in the Jurassic

00:01:12.149 --> 00:01:17.350
Park franchise, but the common conception
I grew up with of the Spinosarus as a bigger,

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:22.659
heavier T-Rex analogue isn’t quite accurate.
Indeed, the Spinosaurus has been the subject

00:01:22.659 --> 00:01:28.049
of considerable recent debate regarding its
biology and ecology, as it might have been

00:01:28.049 --> 00:01:34.030
highly unique from other carnivorous therapods
due to its semi-aquatic or perhaps highly-aquatic

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:40.200
lifestyle. A paper published January 7th by
Dr. David Hone and Dr. Thomas Holtz weighs

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:45.399
the likelihood of a Spinosaurus being a wading
animal that fed on fish near the shorelines

00:01:45.399 --> 00:01:50.579
and in shallow water, or a specialized aquatic
pursuit predator. Their studies were quite

00:01:50.579 --> 00:01:56.189
complex, but to simplify, based on the anatomy
of the fossils we have and related ecological

00:01:56.189 --> 00:02:01.439
data, the wading model is more supported than
the highly-aquatic one. Still, the authors

00:02:01.439 --> 00:02:07.509
note Spinosaurus almost certainly fed primarily
from the water and may have swum, which alone

00:02:07.509 --> 00:02:13.140
would make it incredibly distinct from most
other large therapods… Our next discovery

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:19.320
exposes new revelations regarding the history
of a highly adapted group of aquatic predators:

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:24.620
sharks. For hundreds of millions of years,
sharks of various sizes have been patrolling

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:29.840
Earth’s oceans. And throughout the Cenozoic,
an extinct family known as ‘megatooth sharks’

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:35.310
started to trend towards gigantism — a trend
that culminated in the well known Megalodon:

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:42.790
the largest marcopredatory shark ever to exist.
This species exceeded lengths of up to 15m

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:48.370
or 50 feet, and likely weighed more than 50
tons. But a mystery has long surrounded the

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:54.000
Megalodon regarding the nature of their teeth.
Compared to the teeth of earlier megatooth

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.540
sharks, Megalodon teeth consistently had greater
crown width, for reasons that aren’t entirely

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:05.860
clear. Recently, Dr. Antonio Ballell and Dr.
Humberto Ferrón ran a series of experiments

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:10.990
testing the biomechanics of earlier megatooth
shark teeth compared to the megalodon, and

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:15.800
the results were quite surprising. It seems
stress distribution patterns were remarkably

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:21.180
similar across all models, meaning that the
teeth of Megalodons — while obviously deadly

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:25.151
— weren’t any better adapted than the
teeth of other sharks in their family. It

00:03:25.151 --> 00:03:30.390
seems that megalodon teeth shifted in shape
as a by-product of their gigantism, instead

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:35.890
of a specific evolutionary advantage. Still
wouldn’t have been fun to get bitten by

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:41.480
one, though… Winding back the clock by half
a billion years, we reach the Cambrian Explosion,

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:47.310
a metaphorical eruption in the diversity of
early lifeforms, which, as of January 2021,

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:52.580
we have new information on. You might already
know that the Cambrian Explosion gave rise

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:58.220
to countless bizarre lifeforms, many of which
seem more like alien life than something from

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:03.270
Earth’s past. From the surreal Opabinia,
which might have possessed a flexible trunk

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:08.960
that makes it look almost like a living cartoon,
to the Hallucigenia, an animal so dreamlike

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:13.770
its name comes from the word ‘hallucination’
— it’s a fascinating early milestone in

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:20.449
earth’s biodiversity. And a recent examination
by Dr. A.J. Shore and Dr. R. A. Wood unveils

00:04:20.449 --> 00:04:25.699
a new addition to this pantheon of oddities.
Through the analysis of some exceptionally

00:04:25.699 --> 00:04:30.400
well-preserved fossils, researchers were able
to distinguish a lifeform that possessed a

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:35.849
cup-shaped skeleton with six radially arranged
lobes surrounding what was likely its mouth.

00:04:35.849 --> 00:04:41.259
A reconstruction of this lifeform shows it
was a highly unique form of animal, and perhaps

00:04:41.259 --> 00:04:46.400
a foundational member of the lophotrochozoan
clade — a group that today includes various

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:51.020
types of cephalopods and mollusks… In the
realm of Paleontology, sometimes seemingly

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:58.860
small discoveries can lead to major revelations.
One article from January 6th shows a curious

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:04.169
potential relationship between herbivorous
dinosaurs and the plants that they ate. A

00:05:04.169 --> 00:05:09.900
new examination of fossilized gut contents
by Dr. George Perry suggests plant seeds remained

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:15.080
intact within the stomachs of plant-eating
dinosaurs. Cross-referencing this data with

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:20.069
the distances they likely walked, it seems
possible that herbivorous dinosaurs dispersed

00:05:20.069 --> 00:05:26.139
plant seeds far and wide via their, well,
droppings.Strange though this might sound,

00:05:26.139 --> 00:05:30.819
some modern plant-munchers — like African
bush elephants — play a similar role in

00:05:30.819 --> 00:05:36.349
the, uh, seed dispersal of certain types of
plants. In fact, the dispersal of plant seeds

00:05:36.349 --> 00:05:41.300
within the bodies of animals is so frequent
in current ecosystems that plants often modify

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:47.199
their fruits and flowers to appeal to specific
species. And since some dinosaurs could consume

00:05:47.199 --> 00:05:51.969
even more plant matter per day than elephants,
it’s likely they were highly sought-after

00:05:51.969 --> 00:05:58.271
seed distributers millions of years ago. A
strange, but fascinating discovery… And

00:05:58.271 --> 00:06:03.389
our last article covers a whole new taxon
of marine predator, with specialized teeth

00:06:03.389 --> 00:06:09.229
unlike those of any known reptile. This new
taxon is a member of the Mosasauridae, a group

00:06:09.229 --> 00:06:15.130
of mid-Cretacious lizards that became specialized
for marine life. This exciting new taxon,

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:20.819
called Xenodens calminechari, lived just before
the K-Pg extinction… and stands out from

00:06:20.819 --> 00:06:27.360
all other mosasaurids. As reported in the
article by Dr. Nathalie Bardet and Dr. Nicholas

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:33.410
Longrich, this new Mosasaurid possessed teeth
that were hooked and formed a saw-like blade.

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:38.469
The specialized teeth suggests a previously
unknown hunting strategy, likely involving

00:06:38.469 --> 00:06:44.229
a cutting motion to carve pieces out of large
prey. Or perhaps these unique teeth were used

00:06:44.229 --> 00:06:50.039
for scavenging already deceased marine lifeforms.
In either case, these teeth are unique not

00:06:50.039 --> 00:06:55.529
just among mosasaurids, but reptiles in general.
An incredible finding. And those are just

00:06:55.529 --> 00:07:01.479
some of the major paleontological revelations
of early January. Special thanks to E.D.G.E.

00:07:01.479 --> 00:07:06.280
for inviting me to be a part of PaleoRewind.
Other channels like mine will be releasing

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:10.370
videos covering other months in the coming
days, and I’ll add links to them in the

00:07:10.370 --> 00:07:15.810
description when they upload. And Paleo Analysis
is covering the second half of January, so

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:21.990
check out their video next if you want to
go in order. And as always, thanks for watching.

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:26.770
If you enjoyed this entry into the archive
please lend your support, and like, subscribe,

